## IMPORTANT RULES
1. Extract business rules at the smallest executable unit. If a sentence contains multiple retrievals, mappings, filters, calculations, comparisons, sorting operations, ranking operations, or updates, create separate YAML rules for each operation. Never merge multiple business operations into one rule.
2. Preserve entity names, attribute names, business terminology, service names,workflow names,  and formulas exactly as  written in the source document.

3. Do not replace business logic with generic statements such as:
      attribute exists
      record exists
      value exists
   Instead, capture the complete business condition as specified.

4. Capture complete lookup criteria, including all mapping attributes when multiple attributes are used.
5. Capture complete filter criteria exactly as specified.
6. Capture complete retrieval attributes. If multiple attributes are retrieved, list all of them.

7. Calculation Rules

• Generate exactly one calculation rule for each independent calculation.

• Preserve computation expressions exactly as written. Never simplify or modify formulas.

• Represent aggregate operations (COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX, GROUP BY, DISTINCT, sorting, ranking, and TOP-N selection) as separate calculation rules while preserving aggregation scope.

• When identifying a MAX/MIN value and then selecting the associated attribute, generate separate calculation rules for each action.

8. Every extracted business rule must belong to exactly one section:
      Eligibility Check – rules related to retrieval, lookup, mapping, validation,  filtering, joins, record identification, and eligibility.
      Computation Rules – rules related to calculations, updates, lookups supporting computation, data migration and trigger.
9. If a single business statement contains both eligibility and computation logic, split it into separate rules:
     One rule under Eligibility Check
     One rule under Computation Rules
11. If multiple business conditions apply to a single computation rule, combine them within the condition field rather than creating duplicate computation rules.
12. se YAML lists for fields containing multiple values, such as retrieve and source_attributes.
13. If a required YAML field is not specified in the BRD/TBRD, assign it the value null. Do not invent missing information.

14. Every explicit business action must become an independent YAML rule, including retrieval, filtering, sorting, grouping, counting, ranking, comparison, selection, validation, conditional branching, triggering downstream services, updates, and migrations. Do not omit intermediate business operations.

15. Calculation operations such as COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX, GROUP BY, DISTINCT, sorting, ranking, TOP-N selection, and comparison must each be represented as independent calculation rules. Do not combine sequential calculation actions into a single rule.


16. Extract every IF, ELSE, OTHERWISE, FAIL, SUCCESS, FALLBACK, NEXT SERVICE, TRIGGER, or alternate processing path as independent computation rules. Never omit fallback logic.


17. Preserve all temporal business conditions exactly as written, including date ranges, effective dates, current year, rolling windows, recent years, build years, historical periods, time-based filters, and whether range boundaries are inclusive or exclusive. Do not omit, simplify, or convert temporal expressions (for example, "current year to current year minus 10," "last 10 years," or "recent 5 years") unless the source document explicitly defines an equivalent formula.


18. Preserve all business comparisons exactly as written, including greater than, less than, equal, highest, lowest, adjacent, same, different, matching, non-matching, maximum, minimum, ranking, and precedence logic. When a comparison involves multiple candidates, generate conditions covering every explicitly mentioned candidate. Do not reduce multi-value or three-way comparisons to fewer candidates, and preserve comparison order, tie behavior, precedence, and success/failure outcomes exactly as specified in the source document.

## other Rules:

1. Always represent mappings in the direction input_or_source_attribute: lookup_entity.matching_attribute. The left side must contain the value already available from request data or a previous rule. The right side must contain the attribute against which the available value is matched in the current source_entity. Preserve this mapping direction consistently across all rules.

2. A lookup rule must only perform matching, filtering, and retrieval. Any COUNT, MAX, MIN, sorting, ranking, comparison, TOP-N selection, classification, grouping, or derived value performed on retrieved data must be represented as a separate calculation rule.

3. Every attribute referenced in a rule's mapping, condition, filter, expression, or source_attributes must originate from request data, the source document, or the output of an earlier rule. Never reference an intermediate attribute, alias, collection, object, or calculated value unless it was explicitly produced by a preceding rule.


4. target_attribute must represent the primary output generated by the current rule. For lookup operations, it must represent the primary retrieved output. For calculation operations, it must represent the calculated or derived output. For update operations, it must represent the persisted attribute being modified. If the BRD does not identify one primary output, assign target_attribute: null.


7. For every migration rule, preserve all explicitly stated source attributes, target attributes, source-to-target mappings, target entity identification criteria, and insert/update/upsert behavior. Do not omit the target attribute names when the BRD specifies them. If any mapping or target attribute is not specified, assign the applicable field null; do not infer it.

8. Trigger Rules

•Every downstream service, workflow, fallback, API, or external system invocation must become an independent trigger rule.
•Never merge trigger rules with calculations or updates.

8. Preserve entity names exactly as written. Do not add, remove, infer, or normalize schema prefixes. For example, do not convert part to spriced.part unless spriced.part is explicitly written in the source document.

9. When an entity is described using business wording such as "part entity of Spriced," preserve the exact entity representation explicitly provided in the source. Do not infer a physical database name, schema name, table name, or alias that is not stated.


10. If ranking, MAX, MIN, or highest/lowest selection can produce multiple matching records and the BRD does not specify tie-breaking behavior, preserve the stated logic and do not invent a tie-breaking conditio

11. Do not generate duplicate lookup or calculation rules for an action already represented by an earlier rule unless the BRD explicitly repeats the action with a different condition, time range, mapping, entity, or processing path.

12. Generate rules in execution order based on data dependency. A rule that consumes an attribute must appear after the rule that retrieves, calculates, selects, updates, or migrates that attribute. Never reorder rules solely by operation type.

